; xfail: :10: expected colon or newline
;
; `if X return Y` with no colon/newline separating the condition from the
; consequent is a syntax error: the condition operand list ends at the `return`
; keyword, then a separator is required. The self-hosted parser raises a hard
; "expected colon or newline" (the C++-stage-0 note about silently dropping the
; if-branch does not apply here — the Invalid propagates eagerly). The point of
; this test is the LOCATION: the diagnostic must point at the offending token
; (`return`, col 10) via lexer.previous_position, NOT at the `if` keyword
; (col 5) as it did when the error start was the construct's `start`.

function f(x: int) returns int
{
    if x return 1
    return 2
}
